{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Neuromorphic Computing.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Neuromorphic Computing.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"neuromorphic computing neuromorphic computing","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Neuromorphic Computing.md","fileName":"Neuromorphic Computing.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Neuromorphic Computing","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 02:20\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Emerging Computational Models]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Emerging Computational Models","Spiking Neuron","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Neuromorphic Computing.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Neuromorphic Computing.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"neuromorphic computing neuromorphic computing","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Neuromorphic Computing.md","fileName":"Neuromorphic Computing.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Neuromorphic Computing","heading":"Neuromorphic Computing","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Neuromorphic computing builds hardware around many interconnected, low-power [[Spiking Neuron|spiking units]] rather than using only a centrally sequenced processor. Signals are exchanged as events over time, and the units can communicate in parallel while maintaining local state.\n\nKelleher discusses this architecture as a possible way to reduce energy demands for some neural computations. Its computational organization is distinctive, but a chip's resemblance to biological neurons does not make it a complete model of a human brain or automatically replace ordinary deep-learning hardware. The book also notes the challenge of creating algorithms and software for such large parallel systems.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Emerging Computational Models","Spiking Neuron","deeplearning_mit.